<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="nagata-flag">
  <title>A simplex from a complete-intersection flag</title>
  <p>Let <m>k</m> be algebraically closed, let <m>X/k</m> be smooth, integral, and projective of dimension <m>n\geq2</m>, and let <m>L</m> be ample. We use additive notation <m>NL=L^{\otimes N}</m> and write <m>V=L^n</m>. For positive real numbers <m>a_1,\ldots,a_n</m>, define<md number="no">\Delta(a_1,\ldots,a_n)=\left\{x\in\mathbb R_{\geq0}^n:\sum_{i=1}^n\frac{x_i}{a_i}\leq1\right\}.</md>Its Euclidean volume is <m>a_1\cdots a_n/n!</m>.</p>
  <p>The flag construction in <xref ref="bib-openai-higher" detail="Proposition 3.1"/> and <xref ref="bib-openai-positive" detail="Proposition 3.1"/> gives additive exponent sets whose bounding simplex has volume <m>V/n!</m>. The proof uses formal expansions and applies in every characteristic.</p>
  <proposition xml:id="prop-flag-simplex">
    <statement>
      <p>Choose an integer <m>D\geq1</m> such that <m>DL</m> is very ample, and let<md number="no">P_0=\Delta(1/D,\ldots,1/D,D^{n-1}V).</md>There are finite sets <m>S_N\subset NP_0\cap\mathbb Z_{\geq0}^n</m>, for all integers <m>N\geq1</m>, such that<md number="no">S_N+S_{N'}\subset S_{N+N'}\qquad(N,N'\geq1).</md>Their cardinalities satisfy<md number="no">\#S_N=h^0(X,NL)=\frac{V}{n!}N^n+O(N^{n-1}).</md>For every <m>N,r\geq1</m> and <m>\ell\geq0</m>, if <m>\mathcal M(S_N)</m> separates jets through degree <m>\ell</m> at some <m>r</m> distinct points of <m>\mathbb T_k^n</m>, then <m>H^0(X,NL)</m> separates jets through degree <m>\ell</m> at some <m>r</m> distinct points of <m>X</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>Choose sections <m>\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_{n-1}\in H^0(X,DL)</m> whose successive zero loci form a smooth integral flag<md number="no">X=X_0\supset X_1\supset\cdots\supset X_{n-1}=C,\qquad X_i=X_{i-1}\cap\{\sigma_i=0\}.</md>Bertini's theorem for a very ample embedding gives such sections in every characteristic. At each step the variety being cut has dimension at least two. The smooth integral hyperplane sections are <xref ref="bib-stacks"/> (<url href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0G4G">Tag 0G4G</url>).</p>
      <p>Choose <m>p\in C(k)</m> and a local frame <m>e</m> of <m>L</m> near <m>p</m>. The functions <m>z_i=\sigma_i/e^D</m>, for <m>1\leq i&lt;n</m>, form part of a regular system of parameters at <m>p</m>. Complete them by <m>z_n</m>. These parameters give<md number="no">\widehat{\mathcal O}_{X,p}=k[[z_1,\ldots,z_n]].</md>Write each section of <m>NL</m> in the frame <m>e^N</m>, expand it in this ring, and let <m>S_N</m> be the set of least exponents of nonzero expansions for the lexicographic order that compares the first coordinate first.</p>
      <p>The expansion map is injective. The local ring injects into its completion, and a section with zero germ vanishes on a nonempty open subset of the integral variety <m>X</m>, hence vanishes on <m>X</m>. The frames in different degrees are compatible with multiplication. By <xref ref="lem-jets-least"/>,<md number="no">\#S_N=h^0(X,NL),\qquad S_N+S_{N'}\subset S_{N+N'}.</md>The Hilbert polynomial of the ample line bundle gives the stated asymptotic formula.</p>
      <p>Let <m>\alpha=(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_n)\in S_N</m> be the least exponent of a nonzero section <m>s</m>. We prove the simplex bound by division along the flag. Suppose that a nonzero residual section on <m>X_{i-1}</m> has least exponent <m>(\alpha_i,\ldots,\alpha_n)</m> in its formal expansion at <m>p</m>. If <m>\alpha_i&gt;0</m>, every term of its expansion is divisible by <m>z_i</m>. Its restriction to <m>X_i</m> therefore has zero formal expansion. The local ring of <m>X_i</m> injects into its completion, and <m>X_i</m> is integral, so this restriction is identically zero. The exact sequence for the effective Cartier divisor <m>X_i\subset X_{i-1}</m> permits division by <m>\sigma_i|_{X_{i-1}}</m>.</p>
      <p>Repeat this division <m>\alpha_i</m> times and restrict to <m>X_i</m>. The coefficient of the original least term remains nonzero, so the resulting section is nonzero and has least exponent <m>(\alpha_{i+1},\ldots,\alpha_n)</m>. Each division subtracts <m>DL</m> from its line bundle. Induction gives a nonzero section on <m>C</m> of<md number="no">\left(N-D\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\alpha_i\right)L|_C</md>with vanishing order exactly <m>\alpha_n</m> at <m>p</m>. Since <m>\deg(L|_C)=D^{n-1}V</m>, the degree of its zero divisor gives<md number="no">\alpha_n\leq\left(N-D\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\alpha_i\right)D^{n-1}V.</md>Equivalently,<md number="no">D\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\alpha_i+\frac{\alpha_n}{D^{n-1}V}\leq N.</md>This proves <m>S_N\subset NP_0</m>. The product of the intercepts of <m>P_0</m> is <m>V</m>, so <m>\operatorname{vol}(P_0)=V/n!</m>.</p>
      <p>Fix <m>N</m> and choose a basis of <m>H^0(X,NL)</m> with distinct least exponents, as in <xref ref="lem-jets-least"/>. By <xref ref="lem-jets-weights"/>, one positive integral weight vector makes each of these exponents the unique least-weight term of its expansion. If <m>\mathcal M(S_N)</m> separates the specified jets at a torus tuple, <xref ref="lem-jets-formal"/> applies to the basis sections in the frame <m>e^N</m>. It gives surjectivity for their jets at some distinct tuple in the trivializing neighborhood of <m>p</m>, which is the asserted surjectivity for <m>H^0(X,NL)</m>.</p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p xml:id="anchor-fixed-flag-order">The flag, the frame, and the lexicographic order are fixed for all <m>N</m>. The weights used in the final paragraph may depend on <m>N</m>. They prove the implication about jets at that degree, while the fixed least-exponent order proves <m>S_N+S_{N'}\subset S_{N+N'}</m>.</p>
      <aside xml:id="margin-fixed-flag-order"><p>The fixed order defines one graded family in all degrees, so the least exponent of a product is the sum of its exponents. Exposing weights are chosen only to compare one finite jet matrix. Their dependence on <m>N</m> does not affect the graded inclusion.</p></aside>
</chapter>
